+# FINDINGS
+
+I would not sign off on shipping this vault to mainnet on Monday.
+
+I added only new tests under `test/`:
+
+- `test/MainnetUsdtForkFailure.t.sol`
+- `test/WithdrawalFeeAccountingFailure.t.sol`
+
+I did not change `src/`, `test/UsdtYieldVault.t.sol`, or anything under `test/mocks/`.
+
+## 1. Mainnet deposits with real USDT revert before reaching Aave
+
+### What I found
+
+`UsdtYieldVault.deposit()` uses `SafeERC20` for `transferFrom`, but then calls `usdt.approve(address(aavePool), amount)` directly at `src/UsdtYieldVault.sol:94`.
+
+Real mainnet USDT does not return a boolean from `approve`. A high-level Solidity call through `IERC20.approve` expects return data and reverts when none is returned. The vault therefore cannot complete a deposit with the real USDT token configured for mainnet.
+
+The existing mock does not catch this because `MockUSDT` is a normal OpenZeppelin ERC20 that returns `bool`.
+
+### How I found it
+
+I wrote `test/MainnetUsdtForkFailure.t.sol`, which deploys the vault against:
+
+- USDT: `0xdAC17F958D2ee523a2206206994597C13D831ec7`
+- Aave V3 Pool: `0x87870Bca3F3fD6335C3F4ce8392D69350B4fA4E2`
+- aUSDT: `0x23878914EFE38d27C4D67Ab83ed1b93A74D4086a`
+
+The depositor approves the vault using a low-level call, because that is the USDT-compatible approval path. Then the test calls `vault.deposit(1_000e6)` and expects a successful Aave supply.
+
